window.docContents[45].resolve({"/#Jakarta-Persistence--3___-Entity-Operations--3___6___-Entity-Instance-Lifecycle":{"contents":"Any entity instance can be characterized as being in one of the following lifecycle states with respect to a given persistence context:\n\n* A new entity instance has no persistent identity and is not yet associated with any persistence context.* A managed entity instance is an instance with a persistent identity that is currently associated with a persistence context.* A detached entity instance is an instance with a persistent identity that is not (or no longer) associated with a persistence context.* A removed entity instance is an instance with a persistent identity, and associated with a persistence context, that is scheduled for removal from the database upon transaction commit.\n\nCertain operations of EntityManager modify the lifecycle state of an entity instance. The following subsections describe the effect of such lifecycle operations.\n\n\n\nFormalized SpecificationWith entity instances and persistent identities defined\nin Chapter 2, we now describe their lifecycle states and operations within one\npersistence context.The four lifecycle states defined above become an enumeration:enum phase = {new, managed, detached, removed}\nWe use noIdentity to represent the absence of a persistent identity.\nIn Veil, individual declares a single named value of the specified type,\nand immutable means that operations cannot change that value.immutable individual noIdentity : identity\nTo check the lifecycle of the entities, we now define a relation and a function.\nThe first one, created, tracks whether an instance has been constructed,\nwhether or not it is attached to a persistence context. A newly constructed\ninstance is transient, but created remains true when it becomes managed\nor detached. The function e_phase instead tracks the phase the instance is in.You might wonder why e_phase e = new is not enough and why we also need to\ntrack whether the instance has been constructed.That's because new is the initial value of e_phase e, and the model\nincludes identifiers for possible instances that haven't yet been constructed.\nSo we must define how e_phase e behaves even when the object represented by\ne doesn't exist yet, that is, when created e = false. In that case,\nnew is only a placeholder.For each entity instance, we define a created relation to state whether\nit has been constructed. “Relation” here is a mathematical term used in proof\nassistants such as Lean. It does not mean a JPA relationship.\nA relation records a true-or-false fact for its arguments.\nIn Lean, predicates commonly return Prop. Veil's stored relations return\nBool so their values can be computed when executing the model checker.\nHere, entity → Bool means that each entity has a Boolean value, which\noperations can update.relation created : entity → Bool\nThe function e_phase records each instance's lifecycle state.function e_phase : entity → phase\nThe function pid records each instance's persistent identity, or\nnoIdentity if it has none.function pid : entity → identity\nWith these declarations in place, #gen_state generates the Lean type that\nholds the model's state.#gen_state\nSo far, we have introduced the type identity and named one value of that\ntype, noIdentity, to represent the absence of a persistent identity.\nWe have not yet required the type to contain any other values. If noIdentity\nwere its only value, the persist operation could never assign a persistent\nidentity to a new instance.We therefore assume that the same type contains at least one other value that\ncan represent a persistent identity.assumption [nonempty_identities] ∃ i, i ≠ noIdentity\nTo express the specification's requirements and reason about them in the\nfollowing proofs, we introduce names for three conditions on the model's state:* belongsToContext e: instance e belongs to the persistence context\n  represented by this model.* pendingRemoval e: instance e is marked for database deletion.* identityAvailable i means identity i is available to assign to an instance.These conditions are defined using the information we already record: which\ninstances have been constructed, their lifecycle states, and their persistent\nidentities. Veil calls these definitions ghost relations: they give us\nvocabulary for describing and reasoning about the state without adding\nindependently stored information.The model describes one fixed persistence context. Each instance's lifecycle\nstate is interpreted relative to that context. Constructing an instance leaves\nit in the new state, outside the context. Successfully persisting that\ninstance changes its state to managed, establishing its association with\nthe context.The condition belongsToContext e holds when e has been constructed\nand its lifecycle state is either managed or removed. The check\ncreated e only establishes that the object has been constructed; it says\nnothing about who constructed it. The removed state means marked for\ndatabase deletion while still associated with the context, as defined in the\nspecification above. This membership condition therefore differs from\nEntityManager.contains(), which excludes removed instances.When an operation changes e_phase e to detached, the membership\ncondition is no longer satisfied, so belongsToContext e is false.\nWe use this condition in the identity uniqueness requirement and the merge\noperation.ghost relation belongsToContext (e : entity)\n  := created e ∧ (e_phase e = managed ∨ e_phase e = removed)\nThe condition pendingRemoval e holds when instance e has been\nconstructed and its lifecycle state is removed. It describes scheduled\ndatabase deletion; the model does not represent the deletion itself.ghost relation pendingRemoval (e : entity)\n  := created e ∧ e_phase e = removed\nThe condition identityAvailable i holds when i is an actual persistent identity\nand no constructed instance has that identity. This includes checking detached\ninstances, even though they are outside the context. We exclude\nnoIdentity because it represents the absence of an identity.\nFor every instance e, if it has been constructed, its\nidentity must differ from i.ghost relation identityAvailable (i : identity)\n  := i ≠ noIdentity ∧ ∀ e, created e → pid e ≠ i\nInitially, no instances have been constructed. We give every possible instance\nthe default lifecycle state new and the identity value noIdentity.\nThe after_init block establishes these initial values. Here the uppercase\nvariable E makes each assignment apply to every entity, and :=\nassigns a value.after_init {\n  created E := false\n  e_phase E := new\n  pid E := noIdentity\n}\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Jakarta Persistence\u00093. Entity Operations","header":"3.6. Entity Instance Lifecycle","id":"/#Jakarta-Persistence--3___-Entity-Operations--3___6___-Entity-Instance-Lifecycle"}});